dia manager or  something that. They can check it out and review   it right there and maybe give feedback for how  you can improve the prompts or something along   those lines. Cool. Yep. So, yeah, that's kind of  a completed, , end-to-end system. And just to show   you again, , how simple this is, I want to show  you, , what most people think that a workflow,   you know, looks in an innate in. So I'm just  going to pop out of here, and I'm going to show   you one that, you know, might look a little more  complicated that we're working on. So, you know,   you can imagine the first time you're popping  up X and you see someone talk about a workflow,   you know, you see something this and it makes  for great content and it gets people interested   because it's so complicated and they think it must  be so, you know, special and useful. And while   these things are great, they're not the place  to start. these simple ones are often the most   powerful. So you can see there's only four nodes  here for this entire workflow. and each of these   has a has a workflow too but it's literally you  know a couple steps so let me show you the keyword   tool one what that actually looks when you open  it up and this is the beauty of mcp because again   you're not designing all this complicated logic  in a workflow builder you're just saying hey pump   this data into clod and let clod figure out what  to do with it. So all you're doing is connecting   with Ahrefs. You know, you have a little bit  of code to say, hey, don't pull in all kinds of   other stuff on backlinks and things. I just want  keyword information. And then that's going into   Claude. And you're letting Claude find , what are  the best keywords? Can you group these together?   Can you tell me what to do with them? Crazy,  dude. Yeah. So for me, this has been a total   game changer. And I think that, But I don't know,  no one that I've seen is really thinking about   the simplicity of these types of workflows and  how easy it is to unlock that much value. Well,   dude, thank you for sharing the sauce. I know you  could have easily gatekept this, but you're me.   You to distribute the sauce. Highly recommend  if you're not following The Boring Marketer on   X. he's giving away a lot of these workflows. You  also give away these workflows in your community   that you mentioned, if you want to just pull it  up, right? Yes, sure. So yeah, the community has   been going crazy. In school, we've got, I think,  close to one and a half thousand members now.   And in Slack, we also have another community with  about another thousand people. So we have about   2,500 vibe marketers just working on this stuff  every single day. they're posting, you know,   questions, challenges they might be facing,  sharing really, really interesting workflows.   I thought this one was great. It's a, it's one  of the more complicated keyword research ones,   but it's amazing. So people are just giving  away tons of value in here. And then, you know,   we launched a classroom to kind of help people  work through these step by step. And I'm going to   be making a MCP workflow live within our classroom  here too for our community to be able to set this   type of thing up and experiment with it as well.  Cool. And at boardmarketing.com, you also help   people with this stuff, right? Yeah. Yeah.  So we're basically doing vibe coding and vibe   marketing as a service now. So we're We're making  these very custom to businesses. We're identifying   where people are spending a lot of time manually,  and we're helping them automate this stuff,   get out of the weeds so they can kind of vibe and  take advantage of all this. Cool. I'll include the   link in the show notes. Full disclosure, I'm  an investor in the business. I believe in the   future of vibe marketing, so happy to put my  money where my mouth is. Can I say your name,   James? Yeah, absolutely. Yeah, the cash out of  the bag now. I've been doing videos and stuff, so   people know I'm just not a diamond-headed boring  marketer anymore. Okay, cool. All right, so James,   thanks for coming on. I personally would love to  double-click into more workflows. I think that   this is a bigger topic that needs more time, so  if people got any value from this, please, please   let me know in the comment section. I only want to  create content that you look at and you're , wow,   that was saucy. so thank you James for your time  and you were generous with your sauce and I gotta   ask if you enjoyed this episode please it and  comment and subscribe, share it with your friends   and let's all get that sauce together James thanks  again, I'll see you next time Thanks for having me\n"
